  "full_response": "# **Troubleshooting Guide: Accidental Exposure of Sensitive Credentials in a Public Repository**\n\nIf you’ve accidentally committed sensitive credentials (AWS keys, GitHub tokens, database passwords, etc.) to a public repository, **act immediately** to minimize security risks. Below is a step-by-step guide to mitigate the damage.\n\n---\n\n## **🚨 Immediate Actions (Do This First!)**\n### **1. Revoke the Exposed Credentials**\n#### **AWS Credentials (Access Key & Secret Key)**\n- **Go to the AWS IAM Console** → **Users** → Select your user → **Security Credentials** → **Access Keys**.\n- **Delete the exposed key** (or mark it as inactive).\n- **Generate a new key** (if needed) and update it in your local/config files.\n- **Check AWS CloudTrail** for any unauthorized API calls made with the exposed key.\n\n#### **GitHub Personal Access Token (PAT)**\n- **Go to GitHub Settings** → **Developer Settings** → **Personal Access Tokens**.\n- **Revoke the exposed token**.\n- **Generate a new token** (with minimal required permissions) and update it in your local Git config or CI/CD pipelines.\n\n#### **Database Passwords**\n- **Change the database password immediately** (MySQL, PostgreSQL, MongoDB, etc.).\n- **Update all applications** that use this password.\n- **Check database logs** for any unauthorized access attempts.\n\n---\n\n### **2. Remove the Credentials from Git History**\nEven if you delete the file, Git retains history. You must **rewrite Git history** to remove sensitive data.\n\n#### **Option A: Use `git filter-repo` (Recommended)**\n1. **Install `git-filter-repo`** (if not installed):\n   ```sh\n   pip install git-filter-repo\n   ```\n2. **Run the following to remove the sensitive file from history**:\n   ```sh\n   git filter-repo --force --invert-paths --path <file-containing-secrets>\n   ```\n3. **If the credentials were in environment variables or logs**, use:\n   ```sh\n   git filter-repo --replace-text <(echo \"SECRET_KEY==>REDACTED\")\n   ```\n\n#### **Option B: Use `BFG Repo-Cleaner` (Alternative)**\n1. **Download BFG**:\n   ```sh\n   wget https://repo1.maven.org/maven2/com/m",
